From 3f97b2c8449a55841f7dc2e842fdd4a9d8315a1a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 1aeo <github@1aeo.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 01:49:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 03/20] dirserv: Discard client input received after the
 request

A directory server handles one request per connection, but it keeps
reading while it writes its answer. connection_dir_process_inbuf() then
buffered whatever else the client sent, up to MAX_DIRECTORY_OBJECT_SIZE
(10 MB), before closing the connection with an unthrottled "Too much
data received" warning. So a client that stops reading its answer but
keeps sending could make a relay hold about 10 MB per stream.

Discard that input on tunneled (BEGIN_DIR) connections instead. Once
directory_handle_command() has consumed the request and moved the
connection to SERVER_WRITING, we clear the input in that same pass, so
bytes that arrived together with the request are dropped too; a
still-incomplete request is left in SERVER_COMMAND_WAIT and kept.

We don't close the connection, so a client that sends a stray CRLF after
its request still gets its answer, and we don't stop reading, since
linked connections only notice EOF while reading. A tunneled connection
has no socket, so its TestingDirConnectionMaxStall timeout runs from
when it opened: it still closes a client that keeps sending, and the OOM
handler still covers these connections. DirPort connections keep the
10 MB limit, since each write that a slowly reading client allows
restarts their timeout: there, only the limit closes a client that keeps
sending.

Part of #41339.
